    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 2 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 2.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 9 (Serving District 2)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>333 Seventh Avenue, New York, NY 10001</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:917-339-1600">917-339-1600</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="parent-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Expert Parent Advocacy Tips for District 2
    </h2>

    <div class="tip-card" style="background-color: #f8f8f8; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e; margin-bottom: 10px;">Tip #1: Know Your Rights Under IDEA</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) is the cornerstone of special education law. Familiarize yourself with your child's rights, including the right to a Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE). See 34 CFR §300.101 and New York State Part 200 Regulations §200.1(w) for specific definitions. Understanding IDEA empowers you to advocate effectively.
        </p>
        <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li><strong>FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education):</strong> Education tailored to meet your child’s unique needs at no cost to you.</li>
            <li><strong>LRE (Least Restrictive Environment):</strong> Your child should be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate.</li>
            <li><strong>IEP (Individualized Education Program):</strong> A legally binding document outlining your child’s special education needs and the services the school will provide. 34 CFR §300.320</li>
             <li><strong>Prior Written Notice:</strong> The school district is required to provide you with written notice whenever they propose or refuse to initiate or change the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child. (34 CFR §300.503)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="tip-card" style="background-color: #f8f8f8; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e; margin-bottom: 10px;">Tip #2: Request Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            If you disagree with the school's evaluations, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense.  According to 34 CFR §300.502, the school must either pay for the IEE or demonstrate at a due process hearing that their evaluation was appropriate.  This can provide crucial independent data to support your child's needs.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>Actionable Step:</strong> If you disagree with the school psychologist's report, formally request an IEE in writing. Be prepared for the district to potentially file for due process to defend their evaluation.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tip-card" style="background-color: #f8f8f8; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e; margin-bottom: 10px;">Tip #3: Document Everything!</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            Maintain a detailed record of all communication with the school, including emails, letters, and phone calls. Keep copies of all evaluations, IEPs, and other relevant documents. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to escalate your concerns or pursue legal action.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Create a dedicated folder (physical or digital) for all special education-related documents. Date everything as it arrives.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tip-card" style="background-color: #f8f8f8; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e; margin-bottom: 10px;">Tip #4: Participate Actively in the IEP Process</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            You are a critical member of your child's IEP team.  Attend IEP meetings prepared to discuss your child's strengths, weaknesses, and needs.  Don't be afraid to ask questions and advocate for appropriate goals, services, and accommodations. Remember, the IEP should be tailored to your child's *individual* needs, not a "one-size-fits-all" approach.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>Before the IEP Meeting Checklist:</strong>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Review your child's current IEP.</li>
                <li>Talk to your child about their school experience.</li>
                <li>Prepare a list of questions and concerns.</li>
                <li>Gather any relevant documentation (e.g., private evaluations, medical records).</li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </div>

        <div class="tip-card" style="background-color: #f8f8f8; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e; margin-bottom: 10px;">Tip #5: Understand the Continuum of Services in District 2</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
           District 2, like all NYC districts, offers a range of special education services, including Integrated Co-Teaching (ICT) classes, Special Class programs, related services (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy), and assistive technology.  Understanding the availability and appropriateness of each option is crucial.  Advocate for the *least restrictive* environment that meets your child's needs. See NYS Part 200.6(a) for more details on program options.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>Know Your Acronyms:</strong> Be familiar with common special education acronyms like SETSS (Special Education Teacher Support Services), OT (Occupational Therapy), PT (Physical Therapy), and ABA (Applied Behavior Analysis).
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="common-pitfalls" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District 2 Pitfalls (and How to Avoid Them)
    </h2>

    <div class="pitfall-card" style="background-color: #fffafa; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #dc3545;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #800000; margin-bottom: 10px;">Pitfall #1: Delayed Evaluations</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            The NYC DOE is notorious for delays in conducting initial evaluations. IDEA mandates that evaluations be completed within 60 school days of receiving parental consent (34 CFR §300.301).
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>How to Avoid It:</strong>  Track the 60-day timeline meticulously. Send a follow-up email to the CSE office a week before the deadline. If the deadline is missed, immediately file a written complaint with the NYC DOE Central Region Office and consider filing a State Complaint with NYSED.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pitfall-card" style="background-color: #fffafa; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #dc3545;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #800000; margin-bottom: 10px;">Pitfall #2: Predetermined IEPs</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            Sometimes, schools come to IEP meetings with a pre-written IEP, leaving little room for parental input. This violates the spirit and letter of IDEA, which requires a collaborative process.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>How to Avoid It:</strong> If you suspect a predetermined IEP, voice your concerns immediately during the meeting. Document your concerns in writing after the meeting. Request an additional IEP meeting to address your specific concerns and ensure your input is genuinely considered.  If the issue persists, consider mediation or legal consultation.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pitfall-card" style="background-color: #fffafa; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #dc3545;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #800000; margin-bottom: 10px;">Pitfall #3: Inadequate Related Services</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            Children are sometimes denied the necessary related services (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling) or receive services that are not frequent or intensive enough to be effective.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>How to Avoid It:</strong> Obtain independent evaluations from qualified professionals to document your child's need for specific related services. Present these evaluations at the IEP meeting and advocate for the recommended frequency and duration of services. If the district refuses, request prior written notice explaining their reasoning and consider pursuing dispute resolution options (mediation or impartial hearing).
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 10px;">Q: What is the difference between the CSE and the CPSE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>A:</strong> The Committee on Special Education (CSE) is responsible for students aged 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) is responsible for children aged 3-5.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 10px;">Q: What should I do if I disagree with my child's IEP?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>A:</strong> First, try to resolve your concerns with the IEP team. If you are unable to reach an agreement, you have several options, including:
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Mediation:</strong> A voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the school reach an agreement.</li>
                <li><strong>Impartial Hearing:</strong> A formal legal proceeding where an impartial hearing officer hears evidence and makes a decision.</li>
                <li><strong>State Complaint:</strong> A written complaint filed with the New York State Education Department (NYSED).</li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 10px;">Q: How do I request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>A:</strong> Submit a written request to the CSE stating that you disagree with the school's evaluation and are requesting an IEE at public expense. The district must then either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 10px;">Q: Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
            <strong>A:</strong> Here are some helpful resources:
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>New York State Education Department (NYSED) Office of Special Education: <a href="http://www.nysed.gov/specialed" target="_blank">www.nysed.gov/specialed</a></li>
                <li>The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA): <a href="https://sites.ed.gov/idea/" target="_blank">https://sites.ed.gov/idea/</a></li>
                <li>INCLUDEnyc: <a href="https://www.includenyc.org/" target="_blank">https://www.includenyc.org/</a></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>


